{"name":"Green Gecko","hex":"#cdd47f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"},{"term":"gecko","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any lizard in the infraorder Gekkota.","Any lizard of the family Gekkonidae of small, carnivorous, mostly nocturnal animals with large eyes and adhesive toes enabling them to climb on vertical and upside-down surfaces."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To move in the manner of a gecko; to attach to a vertical or upside-down surface."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gecko"}],"entries":[{"title":"Naultinus","blocks":[{"text":"Naultinus is a genus of geckos that are endemic to New Zealand. On account of their striking colouration, species in the genus Naultinus are commonly known as green geckos. There are nine described species in the genus. Species in the genus share a number of traits that set them apart as quite different from the rest of the world's two thousand odd gecko species, which are generally brown in colour, oviparous, short-lived and nocturnal. In contrast, Naultinus are green, viviparous, live up to 30 years or more and are strictly diurnal. New Zealand has a temperate, maritime climate, and in terms of distribution Naultinus is one of the southernmost gecko genera in the world — some species live in habitats in the South Island which receive regular snowfall in winter. Animals in this genus possess several physiological and behavioural adaptations to cope with these periods of low temperatures and adverse weather."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/GeckoOranaParkNZ_gobeirne.JPG/330px-GeckoOranaParkNZ_gobeirne.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naultinus","wikidata":"Q1552227"},{"title":"Gecko","blocks":[{"text":"Geckos are small, mostly carnivorous lizards that have a wide distribution, found on every continent except Antarctica. Belonging to the suborder Gekkota, geckos are found in warm climates. They range from 1.6 to 67 centimetres in length. They are the most species-rich group of lizards, with nearly 2,000 different species of geckos existing."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Phelsuma_l._laticauda.jpg/330px-Phelsuma_l._laticauda.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gecko","wikidata":"Q16546828"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Naultinus-grayii-N-rudis-scale-morphology.jpg/500px-Naultinus-grayii-N-rudis-scale-morphology.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Naultinus-grayii-N-rudis-scale-morphology.jpg/500px-Naultinus-grayii-N-rudis-scale-morphology.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Comparison of differing scale morphologies of members of the genus; N. grayii (above) and N. rudis (below)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANaultinus-grayii-N-rudis-scale-morphology.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Gecko_foot_on_glass.JPG/500px-Gecko_foot_on_glass.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Gecko_foot_on_glass.JPG/1280px-Gecko_foot_on_glass.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Gecko_foot_on_glass.JPG/500px-Gecko_foot_on_glass.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Close-up of the underside of a gecko's foot as it walks on vertical glass","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGecko_foot_on_glass.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/LizardFemoralPoresRooij.png/330px-LizardFemoralPoresRooij.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/LizardFemoralPoresRooij.png/330px-LizardFemoralPoresRooij.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pores on the skin are often used in classification.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALizardFemoralPoresRooij.png"